CORE
main.cpp
--cpp20
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ unsatisfied requires-clause removes the constrained ctor: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
^.*operator '-' not defined.*$
--
A constructor template whose requires-clause is unsatisfied for the deduced
arguments must be removed from overload resolution ([temp.constr.decl]/1).
CBMC evaluated the requires-clause but left an atomic constraint written with a
type trait (here !is_convertible_v<U, unsigned long>) as an unfolded comparison,
so the tri-state evaluator returned "unknown" and kept the candidate.  The fix
constant-folds (simplify) the substituted requires-clause before evaluating it.
This is the header-free analogue of the libstdc++ std::span(ptr, count) vs
span(_It, _End) selection (see cpp20_span / cpp20_span_basic).
